  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 60af0432a3c869e48a591344a8dafd71, type: 3}
  m_Name: PatPre_5
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  question: "In the clinic waiting room, you pick up a pamphlet about clinical trials
    for patients. As you\u2019re reading about preclinical research \u2013 how new
    treatments are tested before they reach clinical trials \u2013 you learn that
    scientists spend a lot of time studying animal or human cells in the lab.\nWhat
    can lab-based (in vitro) experiments tell researchers about how a new treatment
    may affect patients?"
  explanation: "In vitro tests are done in the lab using human or animal cells. These
    tests let researchers test how a new treatment interacts with real cells. Although
    these tests can\u2019t fully predict how a treatment will work inside the human
    body, they are an important first step in deciding which treatments are worth
    moving forward into animal studies and, if successful, into clinical trials with
    patients."
  correctAnswer: How a treatment affects living cells, such as whether it helps them
    grow, survive, or die
  incorrectAnswer1: 'How patients feel about the possible side effects of a new treatment '
  incorrectAnswer2: How a treatment changes blood flow and heart rate in animals
  incorrectAnswer3: How a treatment is absorbed and broken down in the human body
  questionRole: Patient
  questionType: MultipleChoice
  questionStage: Preclinical
